# Rust Concurrency Guidelines

Rust's type system helps prevent data races, but writing good concurrent code requires more. Follow these guidelines:

## Thread safety

- Use `Send` and `Sync` traits to enforce thread safety at compile time
- Understand which standard library types are thread-safe
- Use thread-safe containers and primitives from `std::sync`
- Be cautious with interior mutability across threads
- Consider using thread-local storage when appropriate

## Shared state management

- Use appropriate synchronization primitives (`Mutex`, `RwLock`) for shared mutable state
- Prefer message passing with channels when suitable
- Minimize the scope and duration of locks
- Avoid holding locks across await points in async code
- Be aware of potential deadlocks in lock acquisition order

## Async/await patterns

- Design for non-blocking operations with async/await
- Use the appropriate async runtime for your needs (Tokio, async-std, smol)
- Understand the differences between blocking and async I/O
- Avoid mixing async and blocking code without appropriate bridges
- Keep Future traits bound sizes manageable

## Parallelism strategies

- Use `rayon` for data parallelism and work stealing
- Consider fork-join parallelism for suitable workloads
- Design for embarrassingly parallel tasks when possible
- Be mindful of work distribution and load balancing
- Use atomics for simple shared counters and flags

## Error handling in concurrent code

- Propagate errors appropriately across thread boundaries
- Use appropriate error channels or aggregation strategies
- Consider panic handling and recovery strategies
- Ensure resources are properly cleaned up on errors

## Performance considerations

- Be aware of contention points in concurrent access
- Consider lock-free algorithms where appropriate
- Understand the performance characteristics of different synchronization primitives
- Measure and profile to identify bottlenecks

By following these practices, you'll create concurrent Rust code that is both safe and efficient.
